/* Rung three of the isolation ladder, and the one that is still unsolved.  The
   same rotation, with one integer that alternates 0 and 1 and gates the good
   state -- the shape a C program gets from a statement counter.  Valid for the
   same reason as the others: the rotation has period four and the good state is
   visited when the flag is 1.

   Rung two (extra predicates, no flag) answers in about 10 s with four pieces in
   the well-founded template.  This one answers at no setting tried: four, six
   and eight pieces, with and without two discriminator conjuncts, and with a
   four-disjunct invariant template.  So ONE integer variable is what separates a
   ten-second proof from none, and why is not known -- that is the open question
   this file exists to pin.  One observation that may or may not be the cause:
   the flag contributes six qualifiers to the template where the two reals
   contribute five between them, and two of those six (`1 - x = 0` and
   `-1 + x = 0`) are the same constraint written twice while one (`-2 + x >= 0`)
   is vacuous for a variable that is 0 or 1. */
Main 0
s.t.
Main (z:int): bool =mu forall (a:real). forall (b:real). (a = real(0) /\ b = real(0)) \/ L6 a b 0;
L6 (a:real) (b:real) (tick:int): bool =mu tick = 1 /\ a >. real(0) /\ b >=. real(0) \/ L7 a b 0;
L7 (a:real) (b:real) (tick:int): bool =mu tick = 1 /\ a >. real(0) /\ b >=. real(0) \/ L6 (-.b) a 1;
